// THE AMDUAT IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of each passage of Budge's
// English (The Egyptian Heaven and Hell, vol. I, 1905, and the Short Form in
// vol. II), written for this archive. Budge's sections mix his own description
// of the painted scene with his translation of the texts written beside it;
// the paraphrase keeps the two apart ("Budge describes…", "The text says…")
// and gives long lists of names only in summary. Where Budge marks a reading
// as doubtful ("(?)", "or, …") and the sense turns on it, the paraphrase ends
// with "Other possible readings".

export const PLAIN_AMDUAT: Record<string, string> = {
  "egy.amd.title":
    "The book's own title: these are the writings and pictures of the hidden palace, about the souls, gods, shades and spirits who live at the western horizon, at the far edge of the thick darkness. The book teaches the knowledge of the souls of the underworld, of the hidden souls, of the doors and roads by which the great god travels, of the hours and their gods and how they move, of the words they say to Ra and the words he says to them, and of the gods who praise him and those who destroy. (One phrase in the list is lost.)",

  // HOUR 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.1.opening":
    "Budge describes the middle band of the first hour. A river runs along it. On it sails the boat of the dead sun god Af, a ram-headed man with a disk on his head, standing in a shrine. Before the shrine stand Ap-uat, Sa and the 'Lady of the Boat'; behind it stand four more gods and the steersman. Ahead of the boat walk two goddesses of Truth (Maat), a god with a knife, Osiris as Khenti-Amentet, the lion goddess Sekhmet, a ram-headed god, four pillar-figures and a leader with a serpent.\n\nThe text above them says: this field is called Maati. The god arrives in his evening boat, crosses a court 220 measures long and a stretch of water 300 measures long, and gives fields to the gods who follow him. The field is Net-Ra and its guardian is Arnebaui. Here the god begins to speak the words that settle the fate of those in the underworld.\n\nBelow, a second boat carries a beetle worshipped by two gods; it is labelled 'the coming into being of Osiris'. Serpents, gods with serpents, two Neith goddesses and a goat goddess go in front. The text says the god passes through this court as a ram and changes his form there. The dead who follow him stay behind in it. Copies of these pictures made as the hidden house prescribes will protect the person who makes them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the words which perform the destinies' is marked as uncertain by Budge: the god may be pronouncing the fates, or simply the words that decide matters, for those in the underworld.",
  "egy.amd.1.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band. Nine apes, called 'the gods who open the gates to the Great Soul', each named. Twelve goddesses 'who unfold the portals in the earth', standing with their arms by their sides. Nine seated gods who praise Ra: three with human heads, three with jackal heads and three with crocodile heads. Twelve goddesses 'who guide the great god', each with her name.",
  "egy.amd.1.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band. Nine seated apes 'who sing to Ra as he enters the underworld' (some names are lost). Twelve serpents that breathe fire, 'who light up the darkness in the underworld' (one name illegible). Nine human-headed gods who praise Ra, lord of the company of gods. Twelve goddesses 'who give praises to Ra as he passes over Urnes'; among their names are Nephthys, Tefnut and Isis.",
  "egy.amd.1.address":
    "Ra stands in the court and speaks to its gods: open your doors and let me into your courts; give me light and guide me, you who came from my own body. I made you from my soul and by my magic. I have come to avenge the blood of my limbs that rebelled against me and to destroy what was made against me. Apes, open the doors and portals for me; let the gods born of my souls come into being for Khepera. Stand up in Urnes, guard your court and keep your places and fields.\n\nThe gods answer: the doors of the hidden West and of the sky goddess Nut are open to you. Light up the darkness, take care of the place of destruction, and come as Ra to Osiris. The apes open the doors, the serpents sing and give you light. The goddess of the hour comes to you; the two soul-goddesses tow you. You have taken the night and will bring the day. You open Net-Ra, the serpent goddesses of Urnes praise you, your word is true against your enemies, and you bring suffering on the condemned.\n\nThe god then fights at the strong doors of the court and tells the gods to bolt them, stay in their places and stand on the banks of the river. They wail when he has passed. The goddess of this hour, who guides him, is Ushem-hat-kheftiu-nu-Ra.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge leaves gaps and question marks: 'come into being for Khepera' may be 'come into being as Khepera', and 'thy secret place Net' is uncertain.",

  // HOUR 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.2.opening":
    "Budge describes the middle band of the second hour. Af's boat sails on with the same crew, now with two serpents called Isis and Nephthys at its front; nothing visible moves it. Four boats lead the way. The first, decorated with the signs for 'heaven' and the Eye, carries the full moon on a support of thirteen sections, the moon of the fourteenth day, with a god holding up Truth beside it. Its text says the god travels in the boats of the earth and speaks as he paddles through this field. The second carries a huge sistrum, the sign of Hathor, between two goddesses, and a beetle called 'the great god Neper'. The third has the crowns of the South and North at its ends, two jackal sceptres and a great lizard from whose back rise the head of Osiris and a White Crown. The fourth has ends shaped like cobras, armless figures kneeling and standing in it, and ears of grain at each end: it is the boat of Neper, the grain god, a form of Osiris. Budge copies several short labels in hieroglyphs, which are not given here.",
  "egy.amd.2.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band. Six seated mummy-shaped gods each with a knife-shaped phallus, among them forms of Isis 'the avenger', Seb (Geb), Khnemu, Thoth and an ape god. A god who 'overcomes the power of the enemy'; Horus of the underworld; a god raising a knife; two ape gods; a god with the heads of Horus and Set, 'He of the two faces'; the crook of Osiris and a serpent called 'staff of Osiris'; a pillar-figure of Osiris facing a lion-headed 'terrifier of spirits'; three goddesses with sceptres and cobras; and four more goddesses, Sekhet of Thebes among them.",
  "egy.amd.2.lower-1":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band: gods with ears of grain in their hair or hands; a god with a knife; three seated mummy gods, one called Amu-aa, 'Eater of the Ass' (Budge's printed text says 'phallus'; his own errata correct it); Osiris Un-nefer with the crown of the South; a god with star-topped sceptres; a two-headed god; gods with inverted life signs, with palm branches (signs of years), with a knife and with the sign for 'year'.\n\nThe text for the upper band says these gods praise the great god when he comes and lament when he leaves. They carry the words of people on earth to the god and bring souls to their bodies. They make the night's offerings, overthrow enemies at their hour, guard the day and bring on the night until the god comes out of the darkness in the eastern horizon. Whoever knows them will come out by day and travel by night.\n\nThe text for the lower band says these gods give the great god the seasons and years they hold. When he speaks they answer and live by his voice; he gives them green plants, which they share with Ra's followers; they give water to the spirits and light fires to burn Ra's enemies. Their field's guardian is Am-nebaui. Whoever knows this is an equipped spirit and is protected.",
  "egy.amd.2.lower-2":
    "The gods of the underworld greet the god as he is carried over Net-Ra into Urnes: hail, mighty soul, Af, guardian of the sky; come as the Living One, Khepera. Cross this field, chain the Hau serpent and strike the serpent Neha-hra. Heaven and earth rejoice. Destroy the darkness of the West. Your boat is yours; those who ferry you bring disaster on Apep. The star god protects you, the doors of the hidden land open, Osiris comes and avenges you. You rest in the West and come into being as Khepera in the East.\n\nThe god speaks to the gods of Urnes: open your hidden doors so that Af can see you and drive away your darkness; draw water and bread; breathe air; do not be overcome by your own stench and filth; untie your wrappings, walk on your legs, stretch your arms, and keep your souls with you. You who live in your forms, speak magic, and hack Osiris's enemies with your swords, who have lasting seasons, estates and bread: make way for my boats so that I can renew your field. You fought for me against Apep; help me travel on to the East. Rejoice, gods of the underworld, for I avenge you and decide your fates.\n\nThey cry out as they row his boat and bring him to rest in the field of the grain gods who follow Osiris. Pictures made after the hidden models, and knowledge of these words, protect a man on earth for ever. This hour is called Seshet-maket-neb-s.",

  // HOUR 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.3.opening":
    "Budge describes the middle band of the third hour. Af stands in his shrine, now with only four sailors, the goddess of the hour and a hawk-headed Horus who ties rope loops on the oarlocks. Three boats go ahead. 'The boat which capsizes' holds three hawk forms of Horus, a serpent 'Fiery face' and two steersmen. 'The boat of Rest' carries a mummy-shaped Osiris, has ape heads at its ends, and is steered by 'He whose face is like a knife' and 'The ferryman who does not rest'. 'The Branch' has lion heads at its ends and carries Osiris with ram's horns, called Shefshef. Four swathed forms of Osiris meet the boats.\n\nThe text above the boats says: the great god travels over the 'Water of the One Lord, creator of food'. The figure here moves the boats that are in the earth and paddles Osiris to this city. The god rests here for a while and calls to Osiris and his followers, and the hidden boats guide him into the field. He paddles on towards the hour Tent-baiu, and the boats go round to the district of Thettu. Whoever knows this will have a home and bread with Ra.",
  "egy.amd.3.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band: an ape on a mound of sand, 'He who is on his sand'; another ape in a vaulted coffer; two jackal gods; a man and a woman holding the pupils of the Eye of Horus, 'He who brings' and 'She who brings'; a ram; a mummy form; Anubis lying on a pylon; a kneeling man who brings the Eye of Ra; a papyrus sceptre 'great of magic'; gods and mummy forms with various headdresses; four goddesses who by their names were professional mourners; and Horus with the hawk standard of Seker.\n\nThe text says: those in this picture have their own flesh, their souls speak over them and their shades are joined to them. They answer the great god, praise him, and weep when he has passed. Their work in the West is to punish Ra's enemy, the Seba fiend, to make the primeval water exist and the Nile flow, and to raise their voices against the fiend. Whoever knows this will not be driven off by their roaring or fall into their caves.",
  "egy.amd.3.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band: the ram-headed god Khnemu; a man praising; four seated forms of Osiris in the White Crown ('lord of the West', 'at the head of the West', 'of the two seats', 'conqueror of millions of years'); five goose-headed beings with knives; a man with a knife between two women; four more seated forms of Osiris in the Red Crown; goddesses with sceptres and with the Eyes of Horus; the god Khetra; and three gods bowed to touch the earth.\n\nThe text says: these beings, with those in the house of Tet, praise the great god; when he calls them they come alive, receive water and their due. Their work in the West is to hack souls in pieces, hold shades captive and destroy the doomed in their burning place; they make fire and flames, and their enemies stand under their knives. They wail when he has gone. The warder of the field is Khetra. Whoever knows this will be a spirit in control of his legs.\n\nBudge adds that Maspero quotes a speech of Ra to the people of this region, but all three copies are damaged and it cannot be restored.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'they receive their due' is Budge's rendering of a word he says literally means 'heads'.",

  // HOUR 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.4.opening":
    "Budge describes the fourth hour as quite unlike the earlier ones. The sun's boat cannot sail the middle band: it must cross the land of Seker. A corridor called Re-stau zigzags across all three bands, with three doors (Mates-sma-ta, Metes-mau-at and Metes-en-neheh). Its text calls it the road of the secret things of Re-stau, by which the body of Seker, 'who is on his sand', enters: the god does not go through the doors, but they hear his voice.\n\nRa and his crew have changed into a boat whose ends are serpent heads, dragged by four gods. The text says: as the great god passes over those in this scene, the flames from the mouth of his boat guide him through these pools; he does not see them but calls to them, and they hear him.\n\nAhead of the towers stand a form of Osiris, the crook of Osiris, Thoth and Horus holding up the Eye between them, and other gods with life signs, among them one with mummy bandages in place of a head and 'Green Face'. The text says these are the hidden travellers on the road of the holy country; they guard the road for those who enter the hidden place, and watch over Anubis as he tows them. Budge's page references point to his plates.",
  "egy.amd.4.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band, beside the corridor of Re-stau. A goddess in the crown of the North (perhaps Neith); a human-headed serpent with two pairs of legs; three serpents crawling, 'who go to every place each day'; the scorpion Ankhet and a cobra 'at the head of the way' in Re-stau, with a god offering to them, 'the guide of the holy way'; a three-headed winged serpent with legs, warder of the holy way of Re-stau, who lives on what comes from its wings, body and heads; the god Ap-tuat before the serpent Neheb-kau, which has two heads at one end and one at the other and lives on what comes from its own mouth; a headless god; and the goddesses of the South and North, who guard the serpent Nehepu and guide it to the hidden thing on this secret way.",
  "egy.amd.4.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band. A large boat with women's heads at its ends, carrying the serpent Hetch-nau, which guards the Aheth chamber at the mouth of its hidden passages and lives on the voices of the boat's two heads. Two standing women, a seated mummy, a lion-headed goddess and a horned goddess sitting with no throne, all made by Horus and standing on the ground of Re-stau. A male serpent Amen, guardian of the passages to the Aheth chamber, and a female serpent with a human head near its tail (her text is unclear). The three-headed serpent Menmenut, 'the hidden image of the Aheth chamber', lit each day at the birth of Khepera: on its back are six stars and fourteen heads with disks, which Maspero explained as the gods of the first fourteen days of the month, carried to the Eye that Thoth and Horus bring. Last, the winged disk of Khepera, the 'envoy of heaven' and the goddess Maat.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The name of the lion-headed goddess (Hen-kherth) is marked as doubtful by Budge.",

  // HOUR 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.5.opening-1":
    "Budge describes the fifth hour, the land of Seker. The sun's boat is towed by seven gods and seven goddesses; the goddesses' label says they tow Ra through this circle so that he may rest in the primeval water. Four 'chiefs who provide food', led by Isis of the West, walk ahead.\n\nMuch of Ra's speech is lost. The towing gods say: the earth opens for you, Ra; you have crossed the Beautiful Land; come to Khepera, and pull on the rope to bring him on. In the middle the ground rises into a hollow mound topped by a woman's head, with a beetle half-visible as it comes down on it: Khepera, who joins the roads of the underworld and speaks to Seker each day. The goddesses welcome Ra and wish him safe passage and the destruction of his enemies. Ra answers the armed gods who guard the food of the West: Isis and the West join you, so that I can protect you as I pass.\n\nBelow the mound lies the 'land of Seker', an oval island of sand which, the label says, guards the hidden flesh. It rests on two human-headed lions, each called Af, set tail to tail, whose work is to guard his image.",
  "egy.amd.5.opening-2":
    "Inside the oval a great serpent lies stretched out, with two snake heads at one end and a bearded human head at the other; wings rise from its middle, and between them stands Seker as a hawk-headed man, who protects his own form. The serpent lives on the magical protection that comes from its mouth.\n\nThe text says: the image in this picture is in thick darkness. The light of this horizon comes from the eyes of the great god's heads, whose flesh gives out light; he guards the flesh of Seker, who is on his sand. After the great god has passed, a sound is heard here like the roar of thunder in a storm.\n\nOn the left is the serpent Tepan, who brings offerings to the great god every day, unseen. On the right is the fire-breathing serpent Ankhaa-pau, who guards the horizon. Four seated gods hold the hidden forms of Seker: the White Crown, the Red Crown, a ram's head and plumes. Behind Tepan four bearded heads crowned with fire rise from a narrow lake, Netu; the gods of the region weep when the boat has passed, and the water here is like fire to those who are in it.",
  "egy.amd.5.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band: the goddess of the West with her arms stretched out; nine great axes, two topped by crowns, whose damaged speech asks for help and promises to avenge them as they avenge him; five guardians of 'those who are drowned' in the underworld, to whom Ra says: guard your waters and lands, steer these to the waters that never dry, and let me pass in peace; a dark mound of sand with a hawk on each side and the beetle emerging below; and a great two-headed serpent Ter, who goes everywhere and opposes the beetle, to whom Ra says: open your coils that seal the earth to protect me, and let me pass.\n\nEight gods, among them Set and Amu, both bull-headed, burn the bodies of the dead with flames from their mouths. A goddess, whose name Budge gives only in hieroglyphs, lives on the blood of the dead while a kneeling man splits a head with a hatchet. Ra tells the gods at the blocks of torture, who hack shades and avenge Osiris, to bind this figure so that he may pass: whoever knows this will pass the goddess in peace. The way into the sixth hour is a door in the lower band guarded by a serpent; beside it is the five-rayed star Budge takes to be Venus.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge marks several names (Satiu, Bath-resth) and readings as doubtful, and much of the text is broken.",

  // HOUR 6 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.6.opening":
    "Budge describes the sixth hour. The dead sun god Afu-Ra again stands in his own boat, no longer towed. Ahead of it sit Thoth as an ape-headed man and a goddess holding the pupils of the Eye. The text says the god paddles through this city towards the place of the body of Osiris, calls to the hidden houses that hold the image of Osiris, and they hear him.\n\nIn the house of Osiris sit sixteen mummy-shaped gods: four kings of the South, four Heteptiu, four kings of the North and four Spirits. Before them a five-headed serpent, 'the Many-faced', curls into an oval, and inside lies the god Afu holding a beetle, sign of Khepera, on his head. Ra wishes all of them their crowns, offerings, rites and peace, and asks them to avenge him and hack in pieces the serpent Apep. The serpent's work is to rise up with this image and travel to every place in the underworld.\n\nThe border above says: this is the hidden path of the West, where the great god is carried in his boat to settle the lots of those in the underworld. Anyone who knows their names, forms and hours, or makes an exact drawing of the hidden place, will be fully fed in the underworld, share the offerings of the followers of Osiris, and receive what his family owes him on earth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'arrange the lots (or, plans)' is Budge's own alternative; 'may ye be adored (?)' and 'with your crowns (?)' are marked as doubtful.",
  "egy.amd.6.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band: nine gods and goddesses seated without visible thrones, among them a mummy with food and drink on his head, Isis of the North, 'Osiris in the heart of the gods', and Horus 'at the head of his field'. Ra tells them: you who receive offerings from your fields and rest there each day, share my provisions; you are masters of your hands and feet, great in your forms and in your transformations; protect Osiris from those who would do him violence. Their work is to give offerings to the gods of the underworld.\n\nNine sceptres follow, topped by the White Crown, the Red Crown and the cobra, each with a knife at its base. Ra tells the kings of the South and North: reap, make your own offerings, let your souls live and your throats swallow. Next come a crouching lion, Ka-hemhemet, with two Eyes on its back; a seated Isis; two more gods; and three shrines, each with a fire-breathing snake, holding images of Ra as the hindquarters of a lion, a hawk's wing and a human head. Their texts Budge gives only in hieroglyphs.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Make ye to be Maat your sceptres (?)' and 'rise up ... on their sceptres (?)' are marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.amd.6.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band: eight seated gods, some crocodile- or ape-headed, and four goddesses. Ra asks them, gods who sit in the primeval water, give light and eat their offerings, to travel in his following, to let his soul be with him and let him rest in his bodies. They live by his voice; their work is to carry souls along, accompany shades and provide water for the spirits.\n\nThen comes the great serpent Am-khu, with four bearded heads rising from its coils: the four children of Horus. The serpent is invisible to the great god; its work is to devour the shades of the dead and eat up the spirits of Ra's enemies. Four more seated gods follow, told to stand though seated and move though resting, and to join their flesh; their work is to watch for the two comings of Khuti. Last, nine fire-breathing serpents armed with knives, named after the great gods, among them Tem, Khepera, Shu, Seb and Osiris: Ra tells them to burn up Khepera's enemies and hack their shades, for they guard the Hidden Flesh made of the primeval water.",

  // HOUR 7 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.7.opening":
    "The opening lines of the seventh hour say: the great god takes his place in the Hall of Osiris and speaks to the gods there. He performs the rites for entering this hall, and moves on against the serpent Apep by the magic of Isis and of the 'Sovereign God'. The gate of this city is called Ruti-Asar, the city itself Thephet-sheta, and the hour that guides him is Kheftes-hau-hesqet-neha-hra (a part of the name is restored by Budge).",
  "egy.amd.7.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. Ra, ram-headed, stands under a canopy made by the body of the serpent Mehen; Isis stands at the front reciting the spells that move the boat. The text says the god travels here by the magic of Isis and of Ser against Neha-hra. If these spells are recited, Apep is turned back and shut up in the hidden place; spoken on earth, they work there too. Whoever recites them will be among those in Ra's boat; whoever does not know these figures cannot drive Neha-hra back.\n\nAhead lies the serpent Neha-hra, pinned to the ground by six knives. The goddess Serqet strangles it with a band and the god Her-tesu-f fetters its tail. The text: this is Apep, whose territory, Tchau, is 440 cubits long and 440 wide; Serqet is at its head, Her-tesu-f fetters it, after Isis takes up the magic of Ser. Whoever knows this will not be one whose water Neha-hra drinks.\n\nFour goddesses with knives, who hack Apep each day, guard four coffers in which forms of Tem, Khepera, Ra and Osiris lie under sand. The heads at the coffers' ends appear when the god calls, then 'eat their own forms' once he has passed. The line above the upper band says: on this road, which has no water and no one to tow, the god moves by magic; copies of these pictures made after the models in the hidden palace protect their maker in heaven and on earth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'He who is with (?) him' and 'halteth (?)' are marked as doubtful by Budge, so who stops beside Afu is unclear.",
  "egy.amd.7.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band (after two last figures of the middle band). A seated mummy god holding a curved object; a lioness-headed goddess; a woman-headed cobra; and Afu-Asar, Osiris enthroned under a canopy made by the serpent 'Life of forms'. Ra asks to pass and to embrace the one in Mehen; to Osiris he says: hail, ruler of the underworld, lord of life, you live, you have magic, your enemies are beneath your feet; flames burn them and a knife hacks them each day; let me pass you in peace.\n\nThree kneeling, headless enemies of Osiris, arms tied behind them, stand before a cat-headed god with a stake and a knife. Three more lie on their backs, roped by the god Anku. Ra tells them: you who rebelled against Osiris, your arms are fettered, your souls are held, your shades hacked; Anku's cords hold you so tightly you will never escape. Then three human-faced hawks in the double crown, and a serpent carrying a seated god, Afu-Tem, who shoots flame at Osiris's rebels and eats their souls.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Grant thou me to come forth on the path by thy spittle (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and the name of the third hawk (Mam or Maat) is uncertain.",
  "egy.amd.7.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band. Horus 'on his throne', hawk-headed, whose work is to send the stars and hours on their way. Twelve star gods, each with a star on his head; Horus tells them: you whose magic has come into being, who rise for Ra in the horizon each day, follow him and let your stars guide him through the West in peace; light your stars in the sky. Twelve goddesses of the hours, each with a star; Horus tells them to fight for Ra, take up their forms and symbols, lift their heads and guide him into the West in peace. These gods and goddesses guide the great god on the hidden way of this city.\n\nAhead of them a great crocodile, Ab-sha-am-tuat, 'Osiris, the Eye of Ra', stands on a long mound from which a bearded human head, 'the head of Osiris', looks out. The text: he guards the symbols of this city; when he hears Ra's voice the head appears, then eats its own form after the god has passed. Whoever knows this, the crocodile will not devour his soul.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the Eye which is in his cheek (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and one goddess's name is erased in the original.",

  // HOUR 8 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.8.opening":
    "The opening lines of the eighth hour say: the great god takes his place among the circles of the hidden gods who are on their sand, and speaks to them from his boat as the gods tow him through this city by the magic of the serpent Mehen. The gate is called Aha-an-urt-nef, the city Tebat-neteru-s, and the hour Nebt-usha.\n\nThe circles are described: these are the hidden circles of the West through which the god's boat is towed; they are to be drawn as on the north side of the hidden palace. Whoever knows them by name will have clothing on earth, will not be turned back at the hidden gates, and will have plentiful offerings for ever.",
  "egy.amd.8.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. Nine gods tow the boat, in which Ra stands under the serpent Mehen. The text: he calls to every circle of the city and hears their voices; the figures of their bodies remain with their corpses under the sand; their gates open at his voice each day and close after him. They tow him and say: come to your hidden image, our god, and to the tombs of Khenti-Amenti; light up the darkness of those on their sands.\n\nNine 'servants', shaped like the hieroglyph meaning 'follower', each with a great knife and a human head hanging from it, seize Ra's enemies and put their heads under their swords. Then four rams with linen bandages before them, images of Tathenen wearing the disk, the crowns of the South and North, and plumes. When the god calls, they answer with voices that are joyful but hidden; after he passes and darkness covers them, they receive Ra's crowns and the soul of Tathenen joins the earth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- In the line 'The images secret of Tathenen, of Horus (?)', the name Horus is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.amd.8.upper":
    "Budge describes the upper band: five circles, each entered by a named door, and a final open door. In each sit three gods on signs Budge reads as 'instruments for weaving' (linen), among them Tem, Khepera and Shu; Tefnut, Seb and Nut; Osiris, Isis and Horus; and bull-, ram-, ichneumon-, animal- and ape-headed gods. The text for each circle is almost the same: those here sit on their weaving instruments, set on their sand, after the mystery Horus made; the god calls to their souls, and from the closed circle come voices like something familiar. In turn the sound is like bees humming, like the swathed dead, like men lamenting, like bulls and male animals, and like people crying for help in terror. The circles are Sesheta, Tuat, As-neteru, Aakebi and Nebt-semu-nifu.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'instruments for weaving' is Budge's reading of the sign the gods sit on; the same sign stands before the rams of the middle band, where Budge calls it 'the symbol of linen bandages', so it may mean cloth rather than a loom.",
  "egy.amd.8.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band: five more circles with named doors, and a final door. The first holds a goddess, a serpent, three 'arrows of Ra' and a ram-headed god; the second, two bearded gods and a crocodile-headed one; the third and fourth, four mummy gods each with his weaving instrument; the fifth, four cobras. The repeated text says the god calls to them and from the circle comes a sound: like male cats crying, like the confused murmur of the living, like those going down to the battlefield of the primeval water, like the cry of the divine hawk of Horus, and, from the cobras, which light the place with the fire of their mouths, like the twittering of a whole nest of water-birds. The circles are Hetepet-neb-s, Hetemet-khemiu, Hap-semu-s, Sehert-baiu-s and Aat-setekau. Last is a door beyond which the god Nu stands over the 'chamber of destruction'.",

  // HOUR 9 ------------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.9.opening":
    "The opening lines of the ninth hour say: the great god takes his place in this circle and speaks from his boat to those in it; the divine sailors join his boat here. The gate is called Saa-em-keb, the city Best-aru-ankhet-kheperu, and the hour Tuatet-maketet-en-neb-s.\n\nThe line above the upper band says: this is the hidden circle of the West. If these figures are made with their names, as drawn on the east of the hidden house, and a man knows their names and places while on earth, he will reach his own place in the underworld, stand wherever the gods whose word is true stand, like Ra's high officials and the great ones of the palace, and it will help him on earth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the mighty ones of the palace' is glossed by Budge as perhaps meaning the Pharaohs, with a question mark.",
  "egy.amd.9.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. The sun god Afu stands in his boat under the serpent Mehen. Twelve sailors of Ra stand with paddles, each named (the first is simply 'the Sailor'). The text: the sailors join the god's boat, in which he travels in his hidden form of Mehen, and will carry him through the horizon to the eastern hall of the sky. Their work is to carry Ra through this city every day; with their paddles they give water to the spirits here, sing hymns to the lord of the disk, and by their hidden words make his soul rise in its forms.\n\nThen come a human-headed hawk on a basket or bowl, a ram god, a cow goddess, all 'at the head of the underworld', and a mummy god of offerings. The text: these give food offerings to the gods of the underworld; Ra decrees bread and beer for them, and they follow him to the eastern horizon.",
  "egy.amd.9.upper":
    "Budge lists twelve gods seated on signs of linen wrappings, each named, and twelve goddesses. Of the gods the text says: they sit on their weaving instruments, in the forms Horus made. Ra tells them: you who are wrapped in your holy wrappings, whom Horus covered when he hid his father in the underworld, uncover your heads and faces and do what must be done for Osiris; praise the lord of the West and make your word true against his enemies each day. They are this god's high officials; their work is to overthrow the enemies of Osiris.\n\nOf the goddesses it says: when the great god calls them they come to life and hear his voice; their work is to praise Osiris, embrace the hidden soul with their words, give life and strength to the god of the underworld as he rises, and speak for him in the chamber each day.",
  "egy.amd.9.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band. Twelve cobras, each on a weaving instrument, breathe fire; Budge gives only some of their names. A damaged line above them, as Maspero restored it, calls them the cobras who light fires for the ruler of the underworld with the fire in their mouths, and swallow their flames when the god has passed. The text says they light up the darkness for Osiris, destroy those overthrown in the underworld, drive back every kind of unknown serpent, and live on the blood of those they hack in pieces each day. Whoever knows this will see their magic and not pass through their flames.\n\nNine bearded gods follow, each holding a life sign and a staff topped by a wriggling snake, led by a mummy god, 'Horus who is over the lakes in the underworld'. Budge names the nine.",

  // HOUR 10 -----------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.10.opening":
    "The opening lines of the tenth hour say: the great god takes his place in this circle and speaks to the gods in it. The door of this city by which he enters is called Aa-kherpu-mes-aru; the city is Metet-qa-utchebu; and the hour that guides him on the hidden paths of the city is Tentenit-uheset-khak-abu.",
  "egy.amd.10.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. Ra stands in his boat under Mehen, holding a life sign and a serpent sceptre. A two-headed serpent with legs, Thes-hrau, curves like a pair of horns, with a goddess in the White Crown facing one head and a goddess in the Red Crown facing the other; in the curve sits a hawk, 'Horus at the head'. A boat holds the serpent Ankh-ta. Then three groups of four: disk-headed archers with arrows, spearmen and bowmen.\n\nThe texts say: the god's divine sailors carry him through this city on the water, and those who live in the water spring to life at the sound of the rowing. The serpent, which is the soil of Seker, travels behind the god into his horizon and enters the earth with him each day. The god in the boat stands in the darkness of the eastern hall. To the armed gods Ra says: speed your arrows, ready your spears, bend your bows and destroy my enemies in the darkness; they drive back the serpent of Neha-hra.\n\nThe line above says: this is the hidden circle of the West, where Kheper joins the form of Ra. Whoever makes a copy as drawn on the east of the hidden chamber, and knows it with the names, will pass through the underworld and not be turned back from keeping company with Ra.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the dead hasten (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.amd.10.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band. A god, Pankhi; a beetle pushing a band of sand, or perhaps entering the horizon, which the text says shows the births of Kheper carrying his horizon to the eastern sky. Two crossed serpents holding a disk between their heads, with two young goddesses in the crowns of the South and North, fingers to their mouths like children; an axe, sign of 'god', with a disk, held by two goddesses. The text says these goddesses gather souls on earth, purify the mighty spirits in the underworld, and swallow their own forms after the god has passed.\n\nEight goddesses, four lioness-headed and four woman-headed, face an ape god who sits on his own tail and holds the Eye of the sun. Ra tells them to make the reckoning of the Eye for Horus, to establish it, and to praise Horus for it; their work is to speak for the Eye and make it shine each day. Eight gods follow, seven with life signs and sceptres and the last a mummy in the White Crown. The text: when the great god calls their names they come alive in the shades in his mouth and follow him to the horizon; they strip the wrappings from the dead, break the bodies of Ra's enemies and order their destruction.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge marks the ape god's name (Af(?)-ermen-maat-f) and one of the gods' names (Amen(?)-heru) as doubtful, and glosses 'his emanation' as 'that which flows from his eyes'.",
  "egy.amd.10.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band: Horus, hawk-headed, leaning on a staff; twelve lakes in which men are drowned, swim face down, or float on their backs, called 'the submerged', 'the swimmers' and 'the floaters'. The original text is full of gaps, so Budge translates a version from Lanzone's edition. Horus says to them: you who have plunged under the water and shine in the primeval water, who swim with your faces to the water and your cheeks full of it, whose souls have lost the air and who beat the air with your hands, move through the water with your legs. Come out into this stream, go down on these waves, fill the great Nile and reach its furrows; your limbs will not perish and your flesh will not decay; you will rule your water and have plenty at my command.\n\nAfter a further lake stand four women each carrying a serpent on her head and shoulders. The text: they live by their heads; they light Ra's road in the darkness, and when he comes out into the eastern hall, Set wakes and travels with him. Last is a sceptre topped by the head of Set, 'Set who wakens'.",

  // HOUR 11 -----------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.11.opening":
    "The opening lines of the eleventh hour say: the great god takes his position in this circle and speaks to the gods in it. The gate by which he has entered is called Seken-tuatiu; the city is Re-en-qerert-apt-khatu; and the hour that guides him is Sebit-nebt-uaa-khesfet-seba-em-pert-f.",
  "egy.amd.11.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. Ra, with horns and disk, stands in his boat under Mehen; on the prow is a disk circled by a cobra, the star Pestu. The text: his divine sailors guide him into the eastern horizon; the star on his boat guides him along the ways of the slowly lightening darkness and lights up those on earth.\n\nTwelve gods march ahead carrying the serpent Mehen on their heads. Ra tells them: you who hold your serpents with your hands, lift your heads, you with strong hands and firm feet, lengthen your stride and join your offerings in the eastern hall. Their work is to bring Mehen to the eastern hall, and they return home once the god has passed through the darkness.\n\nTwo serpents carry crowns from which human heads emerge: the hidden images of Horus at the second door of darkness on the holy road to Sais. When the god calls, the heads appear, then swallow their own forms. Four forms of the goddess Neith, in the Red or White Crown, guard the hidden, unseen gate of Sais. The line above says: this circle swallows its images in the presence of the god Rekh and gives them to those born on earth; whoever makes an exact copy as drawn on the east of the hidden palace will be a well-equipped spirit in heaven and on earth, for ever.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The god's name 'Rekh (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge; 'images (or, forms)' is his own alternative.",
  "egy.amd.11.upper":
    "Budge lists the figures of the upper band. A god whose neck carries a disk with two heads, one in each crown, who stands up for Ra and never leaves his place. A huge serpent with two pairs of human legs and great wings, touched by a god with a disk: when the god calls, the image of Tem comes out of its back, then swallows itself. A serpent carrying a mummy god called 'Her body', who stands above the stars and casts the living to Ra each day. A two-headed god, a ram-headed god, a god in adoration, a god with two snake heads, and seven gods whose arms are hidden.\n\nRa calls them by name: my hidden appearances and secret light give you life, you who go to your shadows, whose breath comes from what my mouth says, whose offerings are on my boat; do what you have the right to do and let your souls follow what I have made. Their work is to bring this god's hidden things to the hidden house each day. Last, four goddesses sit on pairs of serpents: their arms are on the earth and their legs in the darkness; they cry out when the god calls them and do not leave their places, and when the shades appear, the winds of the underworld cease before their faces.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'water at the source (?) of Nu' is marked as doubtful by Budge, and the name of the winged serpent is given only in hieroglyphs.",
  "egy.amd.11.lower":
    "Budge describes the lower band, the place of punishment. Horus leans on a staff, holding a serpent-headed throwing stick. Before him rises a serpent, 'the everlasting Set'. Then come five fiery pits with vaulted roofs, each guarded by a goddess with a knife who breathes fire into it: in them 'the enemies', 'the souls', 'the shades' and 'the heads' are burned. A last, larger pit, 'the valley of those turned upside down', holds four men head downwards. Four goddesses with the sign of the eastern desert and a god Her-utu-f stand by.\n\nThe god's decree: hack and cut up the bodies of the enemies and of the dead who are turned upside down. My father, once helpless, has struck you, cut up your bodies, hacked your spirits and souls, scattered your shadows and cut off your heads; you will not exist, you will be thrown headlong into the pits of fire and not escape the flames of the serpent. The fire of the pit-goddesses is against you; they stab and hack you so that you never again see the living on earth. Horus of the underworld orders their slaughter each day, and the guardians here live on the cries of the enemies and of the souls and shadows in the pits.",

  // HOUR 12 -----------------------------------------------------------------
  "egy.amd.12.opening":
    "The opening lines of the twelfth hour say: the great god takes his position in this circle, the farthest edge of the thick darkness. Here he is born in his form of Khepera; Nut and the primeval water Nu are here for his birth, when he comes out of the underworld, takes his place in the morning boat and rises from between the thighs of Nut. The gate is called Then-neteru, the city Kheper-kekiu-khau-mestu, and the hour in which he comes into being Maa-nefert-Ra.\n\nThe line above calls it the hidden circle in which the great god is born: he comes out into the pool of Nu and takes his place in the body of Nut. Whoever makes a copy as written on the east wall of the palace, and knows it on earth, will be protected by it in heaven and on earth.",
  "egy.amd.12.middle":
    "Budge describes the middle band. Ra stands in his boat under Mehen; at the prow is now the beetle Khepera, in place of the disk of the eleventh hour. The text: he travels through this city by the loyal servants of the hidden image Ankh-neteru. His gods pull him by a rope; he enters the serpent's tail and comes out of its mouth, born as Khepera, and so do the gods in his boat. He takes his place at the edge of the sky at the end of the darkness, his hands seal up the underworld, he stands in the eastern horizon, Shu receives him, and he comes into being in the East.\n\nTwelve gods tow the boat through the serpent, looking back at it. The text: Ra's loyal servants, made by his hands, are born on earth each day after the god's birth; they enter the serpent as servants and come out as renewed forms of Ra. On earth it is an abomination to them to speak the god's name. Twelve goddesses take the rope as the boat comes out and tow him into the sky; they raise gentle winds and moist breezes. The great serpent rests in his den each day; the god calls him Na; he lives on the rumbling of the earth, and the loyal servants come out of his mouth daily.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'tail (or, bowels)' and 'horn (or, forehead) of the sky' are Budge's own alternatives, and the measure of the serpent is broken in the original.",
  "egy.amd.12.upper":
    "Budge lists twelve goddesses, each carrying on her shoulders a fire-breathing serpent, and twelve gods with raised hands. The goddesses follow the god; the flames of their serpents drive Apep away from Ra into the eastern hall; they travel the upper sky with him, please the gods of the West through Ra-Heru-khuti, drive away those in darkness and strike Apep for him in the sky.\n\nThe gods praise him from dawn as he takes his place in the eastern hall, saying: you who bring about your own birth and your own being, heaven belongs to your soul and the earth to your body; you cross the horizon and take your shrine, the gods praise you; go down into the sky and take your two souls through your protectors. Their work is to praise him; they count the gods of the land of Mafket (Sinai, says Budge) and come down to earth before Ra once he has risen on the eyes of mankind.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'count up (or, verify)' is Budge's alternative, and 'They descend (?) to earth' is marked as doubtful.",
  "egy.amd.12.lower":
    "Budge lists the figures of the lower band: Nu and Nut, Hehu and Hehut with sceptres and life signs; eight paddlers and a fire-breathing serpent, Nesmekhef; and ten gods in adoration. The texts: the first gods join Ra in the sky to receive him as he comes out in the east each day. The paddlers drive Apep to the back of the sky after the god's birth and hold up the great disk in the eastern horizon; the serpent burns Ra's enemies at dawn. The ten gods stand behind the image of Osiris, who is over the darkness; the god says to it as he passes: life to you, ruler of the West, Osiris, lord of the underworld; the winds of Ra are at your nostrils and the food of Kheper is with you; you live, and you all live. Of Horus's invisible form in the darkness it is said that Shu lifts it beneath the sky.\n\nAt the end Budge describes the edge of the underworld as a semicircular wall of earth and stones. At its middle is the sun's disk about to rise, joined to the head of the 'image of Shu', whose arms reach along the wall; above it is the beetle of Kheper, come out of the boat, and below lies the 'image of Af', the body of the night sun, cast off.",

  // THE SHORT AMDUAT (Budge vol. II, "The Short Form of the Book Am-Tuat") ------
  "egy.amd.short.1":
    "The god enters the earth through the hall of the western horizon. It is 120 measures (atru) to cross this hall before one reaches the gods of the underworld. The first field is called Net-Ra; here Ra gives fields to the gods who follow him and begins to speak to the beings of the underworld and plan for them. Whoever has copies made of these pictures, the images of the great god himself, will be protected by them on earth and in the great underworld, without fail. The hour that guides the god through this hall is Ushemet-hatu-kheftiu-Ra.",
  "egy.amd.short.2":
    "Next the god takes his place in Ur-nes, 309 measures long and 120 wide. Its gods are the Souls of the underworld. Whoever knows their names will live with them; the god will give him fields there; he will stand with the gods who stand, follow the god, enter the earth, force his way through the underworld, pass through the hair of the long-haired gods and by the 'Eater of the Ass', eat bread in the boat of the earth and be given the front portion of Tatuba. Whoever has pictures of these Souls made as they are in the hidden West (starting the drawings from the West), and makes offerings to them by name on earth, will be protected on earth without fail; so too whoever knows the words the gods speak to the god and he to them. The hour is Shesat-maket-neb-s.",
  "egy.amd.short.3":
    "Next the god takes his place in the fields of the Fighters and paddles over the Stream of Osiris, a field 309 measures long and 120 wide. He speaks to the followers of Osiris and gives them estates here. The gods here are the Hidden Souls; whoever knows their names on earth may come near Osiris and will be given water for his field. The field is Net-neb-ua-kheper-auatu. Whoever knows the pictures of the Hidden Souls in their right forms will be protected on earth and in the underworld; whoever knows them will escape their roaring and not fall into their furnaces; his bread will be with Ra, he will control his legs, never enter the place of destruction, come out in his forms and breathe the air at his hour. The hour is Thentent-baiu.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'furnaces (or, pits)' is Budge's own alternative.",
  "egy.amd.short.4":
    "The god, towed along, takes his place in the secret circle of the West and directs the affairs of the gods there by his voice, though he does not see them. The gate is called Ankh-kheperu and the circle Ament-sethau. Whoever knows this picture of the hidden roads of Re-stau, the holy paths of the Ammehet, and the secret doors in the land of Seker, 'who is on his sand', will be like one who eats the bread made for the living gods in the temple of Tem; he will be true on the roads, travel the roads of Re-stau and see the pictures of the Ammehet. The hour is Urt-em-sekhemu-set.",
  "egy.amd.short.5":
    "The god is towed along the true ways of the underworld through the upper half of the secret circle of Seker, and he does not look at the secret figure of the earth that holds this god's flesh. The gods in his train hear Ra calling to them. The door is Aha-neteru; the circle is Ament, with the secret path of the West, the doors of the hidden palace and the holy land of Seker with his flesh and body in their first divine form. Its gods are the Souls in the underworld; their forms and hidden shapes do not know or see this image of Seker. Whoever makes these pictures as written at the south of the hidden palace, and knows them, will be at peace; his soul will join the offerings of Seker, the goddess Khemit will not hack his body, and offerings made to these gods on earth will protect him. The hour is Sem-her-ab-uaa-s.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'image of Seker (or, the hawk)' is Budge's own alternative.",
  "egy.amd.short.6":
    "The god takes his place on the stream of the lady of the waters of the underworld gods. He speaks to the gods there and gives them control of their offerings; he travels the field in his boat, marks out by his word the estates that are to supply their offerings, and gives water for their lakes. The door is Sept-metu. The secret roads of the West, how the god is rowed over its water to carry out the plans of the gods, their gathering by name, their forms and their secret hours, are not known to men and women. Whoever makes a copy as found at the south of the hidden palace, and knows it, will have plenty of offerings in the underworld, share the offerings of the followers of Osiris, and receive the offerings his kin owe him on earth. The field is Metchet-nebt-tuatiu, the road of Ra's boat; the hour is Mesperit-ar-maat.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Budge glosses the stream's name as 'the Lord of the waters' although its form is feminine; his own errata to this volume replace the name with Metchet-nu-nebt-Tuatiu.",
  "egy.amd.short.7":
    "The god takes his place in the secret place of Osiris and speaks to the gods there. He takes other forms here to drive the serpent Apep from his path by the magic of Isis and of Semsu. The gate is Ruti-Asar, the city Tephet-sheta. He travels this road, which has no water, in his holy boat without being towed, by magic and by his own words, which slaughter Apep. Copies made after the pictures on the north side of the hidden palace protect their maker in heaven and on earth; whoever knows them will be a soul of souls with Ra. Whoever recites the magic of Isis and Semsu drives back Apep, in the underworld and on earth alike; whoever knows this will be in Ra's boat; whoever does not cannot drive back Neha-hra ('Stinking-Face'). Neha-hra's ridge of earth is 450 cubits long and he fills it with his coils. Whoever knows this will not have his water drunk by Neha-hra or his soul devoured by the crocodile Ab-shau. The hour is Khesef-hai-heseq-neha-hra.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The name Semsu is marked as doubtful by Budge; the long form of the book gives the cubit measure as 440, not 450.",
  "egy.amd.short.8":
    "When the god has taken his place in the secret circles of those who are on their sand, he speaks to them from his boat, and the gods tow the one who is in the embrace of the serpent Mehen. The gate is Aha-an-urt-f, the city Tebat-neteru-set. The god crosses the secret circle of the West in his boat, towed by the gods of the underworld. Whoever makes a copy as written on the north wall of the hidden palace, and knows them by name, will be fully provided with wrappings on earth, never be turned back at the secret gates, and have plentiful offerings in the great funeral hall for millions of years. The hour is Nebt-usha.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the holy embrace (?)' of Mehen is marked as doubtful by Budge.",
  "egy.amd.short.9":
    "When the god has taken his place in this circle he speaks from his boat to the gods here, and the sailors join his boat. The gate, through which he passes to the stream of this city, is Saa-keb; the city is Bes-aru, the secret circle of the West where the god and his sailors take their places. Whoever makes copies with their names after the pictures on the east wall of the hidden palace, and knows their names and homes in the West, will rest in his home in the underworld, stand among the lords of the gods' provisions, and be found true before the high officials on the day of reckoning of Pharaoh (literally 'the thrice great house'). It will protect him on earth. The hour is Mak-neb-s.",
  "egy.amd.short.10":
    "The god takes his place in this circle and speaks to its gods. The gate is Aa-kheperu-mes-aru, the city Metch-qa-utebu: the secret circle of the West where Khepera joins himself before Ra, and the gods, spirits and dead cry out over the secret images of Akert. Whoever makes copies after the figures on the east wall of the West and knows them by name will travel round and through the underworld, not be turned back, and flourish with Ra. The hour that guides the god on the secret ways of this city is Tentenit-hesq-khakabu.",
  "egy.amd.short.11":
    "The god takes his place in this circle and speaks to its gods. The gate is Sekhen-tuatiu, the city Re-en-qerert-apt-khat: the secret circle through which the god passes and comes out at the eastern mountain of the sky, 'the eater of eternity'. Its form is in the presence of the serpent Petra, who lives here, and the gods take their places in Ra's train when the birth of Kheper on earth is about to happen. Whoever makes copies after the figures on the east wall of the palace of the West, and knows them, will be one who shares out his offering and a spirit equipped for heaven and earth, without fail. The hour is Sebit-neb-uaa-khesef-sebiu-em-pert-f.",
  "egy.amd.short.12":
    "The god takes his place in this circle at the edge of the thick darkness and is born here as Khepera. Nu and Ammui, Heh and Hehut are present at his birth, when he comes out of the underworld, takes his place in the morning boat and rises from between the thighs of Nut. The gate is Thenen-neteru, the city Kheper-kekui-kha-mesti: the secret circle where he is born, appears in Nu and enters the body of Nut. Copies made after the figures on the east wall protect the one who knows them, in heaven and on earth.\n\nHere the light begins; it is the end of the darkness Ra travels through in the West, and of the secret things he does there. Whoever knows nothing of the secret pictures, in whole or in part, will be condemned to destruction. Whoever makes a copy of what is in the hidden West, and knows these secret images, will be an equipped spirit, come out of and go down into the underworld, and speak with the men and women who live there, millions of times.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the whole (?) or part (?)' is marked as doubtful by Budge, so the condemnation may concern those who lack all knowledge, or any part of it.",
};
